<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nawrahta Minsaw</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1551">1551</a>–<a href="/years/1607">1607</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toungoo-dynasty">Toungoo dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nawrahta Minsaw was a son of the Burmese king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> who ruled Lan Na as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> king from 1579 to 1607, replacing the native line of rulers. After his father's empire broke up he governed Chiang Mai largely independently, and he is remembered as a patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and of Lan Na literature.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nawrahta Minsaw was born Min Tha Sit, the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> of Toungoo and Htwe Hla, a descendant of the Ava royal line who in March 1563 became the king's third principal queen. Raised at the Kanbawzathadi Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>, the prince came to love literature and poetry. On 27 February 1574 he married his cousin Hsinbyushin Medaw, daughter of Thado Dhamma Yaza II of Prome, herself a student of the poet Nawaday, and became governor of Tharrawaddy. In 1576 and 1577 he led a long search in the northern Kachin Hills for a fugitive chief of Mogaung, whom he brought before the king on 30 September 1577; he was rewarded with the title Anawrahta Minsaw.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a>, who regarded Lan Na as the most important of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states, appointed him its viceroy on 28 January 1579, and he and his queen took the throne at Chiang Mai on 2 July 1579. He was the first Burmese-born ruler there, and according to the chronicle Zinme Yazawin the local people accepted the couple. After Bayinnaung's death in October 1581 he served King Nanda, taking Sanda in 1582 and 1583 and sending men, though never going himself, to Nanda's campaigns against Siam from 1584. On 25 November 1595 he defeated the Lan Xang ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nokeo-koumane">Nokeo Koumane</a> and his ally Cao Cetabut of Nan near the city of Nan.</p><p>In early 1597 he declared independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>. The Lan Na, Lan Xang, Siamese and Burmese chronicles give widely divergent accounts of what followed, but they agree that he faced a rebellion in Nan backed by Lan Xang and another in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-rai">Chiang Rai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-saen">Chiang Saen</a> under Ram Decho, backed by Siam, and that he twice withstood Lan Xang sieges of Chiang Mai, in 1599 and in 1602 and 1603. According to the Siamese and Burmese chronicles he submitted to Siam, probably around 1602, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamphun">Lamphun</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekathotsarot">Ekathotsarot</a>, brother of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naresuan">Naresuan</a>. By late 1603 he again held all of Lan Na, as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Siam.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died after ruling for 28 years. His eldest son Tu Laung, who lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, besieged Chiang Mai with a Siamese army against a court faction that had made the middle son Minye Deibba king, and died outside the city. Nawrahta and his queen were poets, and the Zinme Yazawin preserves some of their yadu poems, a form whose golden age, in the words of the historian Ni Ni Myint, was the 16th and early 17th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nawrahta Minsaw” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Burmese kings first ruled through Lan Na vassals, including Queen Wisutthithewi, and from 1579 through Bayinnaung's son <b>Nawrahta Minsaw</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Taungoo, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Chiang Mai, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.93,96.43&amp;year=1587" aria-label="Taungoo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7159 1462 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nawrahta Minsaw"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7609 1732h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7609 1732h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.93,96.43&amp;year=1587">What was here in 1587? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
